statementMd:
*In this problem you will have to deal with a real algorithm that is used in the VK social network.*

As in any other company that creates high-loaded websites, the VK developers have to deal with request statistics regularly. An important indicator reflecting the load of the site is the mean number of requests for a certain period of time of $*T*$ seconds (for example, $*T* = 60 *seconds* = 1 *min*$ and $*T* = 86400 *seconds* = 1 *day*$). For example, if this value drops dramatically, that shows that the site has access problem. If this value grows, that may be a reason to analyze the cause for the growth and add more servers to the website if it is really needed.

However, even such a natural problem as counting the mean number of queries for some period of time can be a challenge when you process the amount of data of a huge social network. That's why the developers have to use original techniques to solve problems approximately, but more effectively at the same time.

Let's consider the following formal model. We have a service that works for $*n*$ seconds. We know the number of queries to this resource $*a*_{*t*}$ at each moment of time $*t*$ ($1 ≤ *t* ≤ *n*$). Let's formulate the following algorithm *of calculating the mean with exponential decay*. Let $*c*$ be some real number, strictly larger than one.

```
// setting this constant value correctly can adjust
// the time range for which statistics will be calculated
double c = some constant value;

// as the result of the algorithm's performance this variable will contain
// the mean number of queries for the last
// T seconds by the current moment of time
double mean = 0.0;

for t = 1..n: // at each second, we do the following:
    // at is the number of queries that came at the last second;
    mean = (mean + at / T) / c;
```

Thus, the mean variable is recalculated each second using the number of queries that came at that second. We can make some mathematical calculations and prove that choosing the value of constant $*c*$ correctly will make the value of `mean` not very different from the real mean value $*a*_{*x*}$ at $*t* - *T* + 1 ≤ *x* ≤ *t*$.

The advantage of such approach is that it only uses the number of requests at the current moment of time and doesn't require storing the history of requests for a large time range. Also, it considers the recent values with the weight larger than the weight of the old ones, which helps to react to dramatic change in values quicker.

However before using the new theoretical approach in industrial programming, there is an obligatory step to make, that is, to test its credibility practically on given test data sets. Your task is to compare the data obtained as a result of the work of an approximate algorithm to the real data.

You are given $*n*$ values $*a*_{*t*}$, integer $*T*$ and real number $*c*$. Also, you are given $*m*$ moments $*p*_{*j*}$ ($1 ≤ *j* ≤ *m*$), where we are interested in the mean value of the number of queries for the last $*T*$ seconds. Implement two algorithms. The first one should calculate the required value by definition, i.e. by the formula ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/3b7e6a2a96e83f82fad8d5ae0b0196625482460a.png). The second algorithm should calculate the mean value as is described above. Print both values and calculate the relative error of the second algorithm by the formula ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/ce00b789c658da21a80813906edd50e449ca0654.png), where $*approx*$ is the approximate value, obtained by the second algorithm, and $*real*$ is the exact value obtained by the first algorithm.

## Input

The first line contains integer $*n*$ ($1 ≤ *n* ≤ 2·10^{5}$), integer $*T*$ ($1 ≤ *T* ≤ *n*$) and real number $*c*$ ($1 < *c* ≤ 100$) — the time range when the resource should work, the length of the time range during which we need the mean number of requests and the coefficient $*c*$ of the work of approximate algorithm. Number $*c*$ is given with exactly six digits after the decimal point.

The next line contains $*n*$ integers $*a*_{*t*}$ ($1 ≤ *a*_{*t*} ≤ 10^{6}$) — the number of queries to the service at each moment of time.

The next line contains integer $*m*$ ($1 ≤ *m* ≤ *n*$) — the number of moments of time when we are interested in the mean number of queries for the last $*T*$ seconds.

The next line contains $*m*$ integers $*p*_{*j*}$ ($*T* ≤ *p*_{*j*} ≤ *n*$), representing another moment of time for which we need statistics. Moments $*p*_{*j*}$ are strictly increasing.

## Output

Print $*m*$ lines. The $*j*$-th line must contain three numbers $*real*$, $*approx*$ and $*error*$, where:

- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/b658ef1b555d4a43335450db8ab1cfbcf0bfd944.png) is the real mean number of queries for the last $*T*$ seconds;
- $*approx*$ is calculated by the given algorithm and equals $*mean*$ at the moment of time $*t* = *p*_{*j*}$ (that is, after implementing the $*p*_{*j*}$-th iteration of the cycle);
- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/963758ed5561398191a4d0771088a335fcdcc2d3.png) is the relative error of the approximate algorithm.

The numbers you printed will be compared to the correct numbers with the relative or absolute error $10^{ - 4}$. It is recommended to print the numbers with at least five digits after the decimal point.

statementZhMd:
*在本题中，你需要处理一个 VK 社交网络中实际使用的算法。*

与其他创建高负载网站的公司一样，VK 的开发者需要定期处理请求统计数据。反映网站负载的重要指标之一，是某段 $*T*$ 秒时间内的平均请求数（例如，$*T* = 60 *seconds* = 1 *min*$，以及 $*T* = 86400 *seconds* = 1 *day*$）。例如，如果这个值急剧下降，说明网站可能出现了访问问题。如果这个值上升，则可能需要分析增长的原因，并在确有必要时为网站增加服务器。

然而，即使是计算某段时间内的平均请求数这样自然的问题，在处理大型社交网络产生的海量数据时也可能成为挑战。因此，开发者必须使用一些特殊技术，在更高效的同时近似地解决问题。

考虑如下形式化模型。我们有一个运行了 $*n*$ 秒的服务。我们知道每个时刻 $*t*$（$1 ≤ *t* ≤ *n*$）对该资源发起的请求数 $*a*_{*t*}$。下面介绍一种使用指数衰减计算平均值的算法。设 $*c*$ 为某个严格大于 1 的实数。

```
// setting this constant value correctly can adjust
// the time range for which statistics will be calculated
double c = some constant value;

// as the result of the algorithm's performance this variable will contain
// the mean number of queries for the last
// T seconds by the current moment of time
double mean = 0.0;

for t = 1..n: // at each second, we do the following:
    // at is the number of queries that came at the last second;
    mean = (mean + at / T) / c;
```

因此，`mean` 变量会在每一秒使用该秒到达的请求数重新计算。通过一些数学计算可以证明，正确选择常数 $*c*$ 的值后，`mean` 的值将不会与时刻 $*t* - *T* + 1 ≤ *x* ≤ *t*$ 对应的真实平均值 $*a*_{*x*}$ 相差太多。

这种方法的优点在于，它只使用当前时刻的请求数，不需要存储较长时间范围内的请求历史。此外，它会为较新的值赋予比旧值更大的权重，这有助于更快地响应数值的剧烈变化。

然而，在将这一新的理论方法应用于工业级程序之前，必须进行一个必要步骤，即使用给定的测试数据集在实践中检验其可信度。你的任务是将近似算法得到的数据与真实数据进行比较。

给定 $*n*$ 个值 $*a*_{*t}$、整数 $*T*$ 和实数 $*c*$。另外，给定 $*m*$ 个时刻 $*p*_{*j}$（$1 ≤ *j* ≤ *m*$），我们希望知道这些时刻前 $*T*$ 秒内的请求数平均值。请实现两种算法。第一种算法应根据定义计算所需的值，即使用公式 ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/3b7e6a2a96e83f82fad8d5ae0b0196625482460a.png) 计算。第二种算法应按照上文所述计算平均值。输出这两个值，并根据公式 ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/ce00b789c658da21a80813906edd50e449ca0654.png) 计算第二种算法的相对误差，其中 $*approx*$ 是第二种算法得到的近似值，$*real*$ 是第一种算法得到的精确值。

## 输入格式

第一行包含整数 $*n*$（$1 ≤ *n* ≤ 2·10^{5}$）、整数 $*T*$（$1 ≤ *T* ≤ *n*$）和实数 $*c*$（$1 < *c* ≤ 100$）——资源应运行的时间范围、需要计算平均请求数的时间范围长度，以及近似算法的工作系数 $*c*$。数字 $*c*$ 的小数点后恰好有六位数字。

下一行包含 $*n*$ 个整数 $*a*_{*t}$（$1 ≤ *a*_{*t} ≤ 10^{6}$）——每个时刻对服务发起的请求数。

下一行包含整数 $*m*$（$1 ≤ *m* ≤ *n*$）——我们希望查询前 $*T*$ 秒内平均请求数的时刻数量。

下一行包含 $*m*$ 个整数 $*p*_{*j}$（$*T* ≤ *p*_{*j} ≤ *n*$），表示需要统计的时刻。$*p*_{*j}$ 严格递增。

## 输出格式

输出 $*m*$ 行。第 $*j*$ 行应包含三个数 $*real*$、$*approx*$ 和 $*error*$，其中：

- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/b658ef1b555d4a43335450db8ab1cfbcf0bfd944.png) 是前 $*T*$ 秒内的真实平均请求数；
- $*approx*$ 由给定算法计算得到，等于时刻 $*t* = *p*_{*j}$ 的 `mean` 值（即执行循环的第 $*p*_{*j}$ 次迭代后得到的值）；
- ![image](https://espresso.codeforces.com/963758ed5561398191a4d0771088a335fcdcc2d3.png) 是近似算法的相对误差。

你输出的数值将以 $10^{ - 4}$ 的相对误差或绝对误差进行校验。建议输出至少包含五位小数。
